{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2523 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "135347f9c346c78a5d4bc193c960951add31c6d2031b94f014df4a7e6108d133" :edb/db-before-t 2522 :edb/db-after-t 2523 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535835 50 #inst "2026-10-09T08:28:30.610Z" 13194139535835 true] [17592186092263 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369077 13194139535835 true] [17592186119666 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369056 13194139535835 true] [17592186120206 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535835 true] [17592186120206 1100 "Find the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t = \\frac{π}{3}$, given that\n$r(t)|= ⟨\\cos 3t, 4t,-\\sin 3t⟩; T(t)|= \\frac{1}{5}⟨ - 3\\sin 3t, 4,-3\\cos 3t⟩; N(t)|= ⟨ - \\cos 3t, 0, \\sin 3t⟩$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." 13194139535835 false] [17592186120206 1100 "Find the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,4t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t⟩ \\\\ T(t) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,4,\\,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t⟩ \\\\ N(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." 13194139535835 true] [17592186120206 1145 "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,4,\\,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})⟩=\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,3⟩ \\\\ N(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})⟩=⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is\n\n$$\np=r(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,4(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})⟩=⟨-1,\\frac{4\\pi }{3},0⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ and the principal normal $N(\\frac{\\pi }{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\times N(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,3⟩\\times ⟨1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,j-4\\,k) \\\\  & =⟨0,\\frac{3}{5},-\\frac{4}{5}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨-1,\\frac{4\\pi }{3},0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\frac{3}{5},-\\frac{4}{5}⟩| \\\\  & =|\\,0+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}+0\\,| \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535835 true] [17592186124375 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369154 13194139535835 true] [17592186124450 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369070 13194139535835 true] [17592186124464 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369140 13194139535835 true] [17592186124488 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369168 13194139535835 true] [17592186124495 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369182 13194139535835 true] [17592186124596 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369105 13194139535835 true] [17592186129114 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369175 13194139535835 true] [17592186129114 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369196 13194139535835 true] [17592186129126 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369189 13194139535835 true] [17592186147389 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369203 13194139535835 true] [17592186