ldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4xy-3{y}^{2} & 2{x}^{2}-6xy\\end{aligned}],$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4xy-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}-6xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy-3{y}^{2})=4y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-6xy)=4x-6y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy-3{y}^{2})=4x-6y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-6xy)=-6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4y & 4x-6y \\\\ 4x-6y & -6x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(1,1)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=4\\cdot (-6)-(-2)\\cdot (-2)=-28.\n$$" 13194139535835 true] [17592186364969 1145 "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTh