e] [17592186369029 1110 #uuid "01a580a8-b326-5f5d-842f-75c055c84bbe" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369030 1034 "0.1" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369030 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369030 1111 #uuid "f0b8edcc-96fd-5f4f-b5d1-f14e6777bf16" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369031 1034 "0.25" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369031 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369031 1111 #uuid "208d15e2-290f-5d19-90a7-cac6761f6e29" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369032 1034 "0.15" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369032 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369032 1111 #uuid "e4b781f1-21d5-516f-a56f-2aba6d4471d1" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369033 1034 "0.35" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369033 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369033 1111 #uuid "91fe8502-88a9-5946-bb20-f9f217bfbc2a" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369034 1034 "0.45" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369034 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369034 1111 #uuid "bfbe2db0-dc18-5d7c-840a-19ecaec379f4" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369035 1017 #edb/ref 17592186369036 13194139535835 true] [17592186369035 1089 "q-111083" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369035 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535835 true] [17592186369035 1100 "|  | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ |\n\nCompute $P(X=1\\,\\,\\text{or}\\,\\,Y<2)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139535835 true] [17592186369035 1113 #uuid "698a78eb-ef71-5b2a-bf3e-b4f41a43c1f0" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369035 1145 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1\\,\\,\\text{or}\\,\\,Y<2) & =P((X,Y)\\in {(0,1),(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)}) \\\\  & =P((X,Y)=(0,1))+P((X,Y)=(1,1)) \\\\  & +P((X,Y)=(1,2))+P((X,Y)=(2,1)) \\\\  & =f(0,1)+f(1,1)+f(1,2)+f(2,1) \\\\  & =0.1+0.25+0.1+0.25 \\\\  & =0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369036 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369037 13194139535835 true] [17592186369036 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369038 13194139535835 true] [17592186369036 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369039 13194139535835 true] [17592186369036 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369040 13194139535835 true] [17592186369036 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369041 13194139535835 true] [17592186369036 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535835 true] [17592186369036 1041 "selection" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369036 1104 #edb/ref 17592186369038 13194139535835 true] [17592186369036 1110 #uuid "db0d6d73-e8ba-5736-8685-c62c1ec3e267" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369037 1034 "0.6" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369037 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369037 1111 #uuid "8718fa61-5101-5d42-8ff5-a4623b342288" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369038 1034 "0.7" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369038 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369038 1111 #uuid "5b01b0ad-f144-5698-8cca-3d3fac82494a" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369039 1034 "0.25" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369039 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369039 1111 #uuid "eaa2ed43-626c-52f6-bd69-46f64fcc1498" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369040 1034 "0.35" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369040 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369040 1111 #uuid "b76e551d-542c-5953-b371-3eb6aa5c1290" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369041 1034 "0.65" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369041 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369041 1111 #uuid "91dc9fea-f836-5738-a361-7ddb57dd111c" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369042 1017 #edb/ref 17592186369043 13194139535835 true] [17592186369042 1089 "q-118552" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369042 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535835 true] [17592186369042 1100 "The population $y(t)$ of Weddell seals in a certain region of Antarctica follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1.2y-0.002{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=100,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years. According to this model, what is the maximum possible seal population?" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369042 1113 #uuid "21e1d7d5-e8e8-5e32-9fd2-ba17c7038c72" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369042 1145 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =1.2y-0.002{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.002y(600-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=600.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $600,$ and we conclude that the number of seals cannot exceed $600.$" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369043 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369044 13194139535835 true] [17592186369043 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369045 13194139535835 true] [17592186369043 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369046 13194139535835 true] [17592186369043 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369047 13194139535835 true] [17592186369043 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369048 13194139535835 true] [17592186369043 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535835 true] [17592186369043 1041 "selection" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369043 1104 #edb/ref 17592186369046 13194139535835 true] [17592186369043 1110 #uuid "2458073f-13ac-5d6c-aef7-398cb69aef1f" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369044 1034 "1\\,200" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369044 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369044 1111 #uuid "956c66aa-326f-5a06-be61-c05003bfa3eb" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369045 1034 "500" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369045 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369045 1111 #uuid "0ca48a55-b183-5ba6-bab7-3d758f101f7d" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369046 1034 "600" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369046 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369046 1111 #uuid "74a3ebf5-b294-5497-baf7-ae35b4e1f967" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369047 1034 "300" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369047 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369047 1111 #uuid "d8d8fc59-52e9-5ee5-9939-6579d13a3e20" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369048 1034 "850" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369048 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369048 1111 #uuid "8800b3d1-7cf8-51e0-944b-60d87f9f0afa" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369049 1017 #edb/ref 17592186369050 13194139535835 true] [17592186369049 1089 "q-126031" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369049 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535835 true] [17592186369049 1100 "Compute $P(X>2)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369049 1113 #uuid "e4f38637-761c-59db-ada2-8df3d6501eff" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369049 1145 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =P(2<X<\\infty ) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{x+1}]}_{2}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369050 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369051 13194139535835 true] [17592186369050 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369052 13194139535835 true] [17592186369050 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369053 13194139535835 true] [17592186369050 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369054 13194139535835 true] [17592186369050 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369055 13194139535835 true] [17592186369050 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535835 true] [17592186369050 1041 "selection" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369050 1104 #edb/ref 17592186369054 13194139535835 true] [17592186369050 1110 #uuid "15aa5794-c6e7-58c5-875f-8b6c68d5310f" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369051 1034 "\\frac{2}{3}" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369051 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369051 1111 #uuid "82dbdc2f-390b-5464-afc5-a6e0401eb8e4" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369052 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369052 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369052 1111 #uuid "ea11022f-b894-57fc-8592-605dd62043ce" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369053 1034 "\\frac{1}{6}" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369053 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369053 1111 #uuid "ec4c754b-eeac-5261-b97f-ae67f63753ac" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369054 1034 "\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369054 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369054 1111 #uuid "10c5d6ce-a270-5504-870e-d1547fa6d4bc" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369055 1034 "\\frac{1}{9}" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369055 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369055 1111 #uuid "ce84bfcc-1740-5f0a-9e05-b303c55f346b" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369056 1017 #edb/ref 17592186369057 13194139535835 true] [17592186369056 1089 "q-127319" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369056 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535835 true] [17592186369056 1100 "Find the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ T(t) & =⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ N(t) & =⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." 13194139535835 true] [17592186369056 1113 #uuid "6bc59286-96e6-53b4-9925-d6577fe6687e" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369056 1145 "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩=⟨0,0,1⟩ \\\\ N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩=⟨0,-1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\np=r(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩=⟨2,1,0⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and the principal normal $N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =⟨0,0,1⟩\\times ⟨0,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =i \\\\  & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨2,1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0,0⟩| \\\\  & =|2+0+0| \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369057 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369058 13194139535835 true] [17592186369057 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369059 13194139535835 true] [17592186369057 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369060 13194139535835 true] [17592186369057 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369061 13194139535835 tru