0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}.\n$$" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369063 1113 #uuid "6b76a4e9-0550-5e9b-86c9-b6dc6aa2f3d3" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369063 1145 "Given a matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $k$ is a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)^{k}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-\\lambda I)^{k-1}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to $\\lambda =5,$ we need to find a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-5I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-5I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A-5I)^{3}v=0.$ We're given that\n\n$$\n(A-5I)^{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nThis means that *any* vector in ${R}^{3}$ is a solution to $(A-5I)^{3}v=0.$ Therefore, the solution to $(A-5I)^{3}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A-5I)^{2}v=0.$ We have\n\n$$\n(A-5I)^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nThis means that the solution to $(A-5I)^{2}v=0$ is ${v}_{1}=0,$ where ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are free variables. Therefore, the solution to $(A-5I)^{2}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the solutions, we see that the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ satisfies $(A-5I)^{3}v=0$ but does *not* satisfy $(A-5I)^{2}v=0.$ \n\nHence, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ of $A.$" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369064 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369065 13194139535835 true] [17592186369064 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369066 13194139535835 true] [17592186369064 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369067 13194139535835 true] [17592186369064 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369068 13194139535835 true] [17592186369064 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369069 13194139535835 true] [17592186369064 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535835 true] [17592186369064 1041 "selection" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369064 1104 #edb/ref 17592186369067 13194139535835 true] [17592186369064 1110 #uuid "ccc5fe84-e0eb-5c89-8ed3-a3003bceca78" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369065 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369065 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369065 1111 #uuid "4836cd99-8949-51d8-b090-f062ac317564" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369066 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369066 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369066 1111 #uuid "c36ebd00-683a-5e3c-9a28-ade34577392c" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369067 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369067 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369067 1111 #uuid "a17252a6-31dc-5d50-a563-9e0f116a754e" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369068 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369068 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369068 1111 #uuid "75299007-da8f-58fd-a7f1-9b1339182643" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369069 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369069 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369069 1111 #uuid "ff4a5a41-6436-54f6-99ed-8854f0ba75d7" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369070 1017 #edb/ref 17592186369071 13194139535835 true] [17592186369070 1089 "q-140842" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369070 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535835 true] [17592186369070 1100 "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A+8I)=2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A+8I$ is singular\n- $\\lambda =-8$ is an eigenvalue of $A$\n- $\\text{rank}(A+8I)=2$" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369070 1113 #uuid "499f8d57-6ade-5414-a50c-fa707a48dd35" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369070 1145 "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements I is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A+8I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $A+8I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that if $M=A+8I$ is singular, then $(A+8I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =-8$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A+8I) & =3-\\text{nullity}(A+8I) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369071 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369072 13194139535835 true] [17592186369071 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369073 13194139535835 true] [17592186369071 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369074 13194139535835 true] [17592186369071 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369075 13194139535835 true] [17592186369071 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369076 13194139535835 true] [17592186369071 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535835 true] [17592186369071 1041 "selection" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369071 1104 #edb/ref 17592186369076 13194139535835 true] [17592186369071 1110 #uuid "266295c1-cb88-5e0b-a120-392a3af6ca3f" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369072 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369072 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535835 true] [17592186369072 1111 #uuid "3f32c1ca-5ea4-5bc6-9eef-a9a7617398aa" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369073 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369073 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535835 true] [17592186369073 1111 #uuid "cd71f260-59ae-5c57-be24-c4d8d32e148b" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369074 1034 "I only" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369074 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535835 true] [17592186369074 1111 #uuid "d12ea080-cff9-5044-ac40-ef37c623517c" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369075 1034 "III only" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369075 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535835 true] [17592186369075 1111 #uuid "fe147d5c-ed6c-55f7-8a2f-0f6524262929" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369076 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369076 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535835 true] [17592186369076 1111 #uuid "c7dc72ae-b933-5509-95b6-26b3ca2355ba" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369077 1017 #edb/ref 17592186369078 13194139535835 true] [17592186369077 1089 "q-143876" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369077 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535835 true] [17592186369077 1100 "Three baskets, $A,B,$ and $C,$ contain pears. It is known that $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the pears from basket $A$ and $\\frac{1}{5}$ of those from basket $B$ are ripe. A basket is selected at random, and a pear is chosen at random from the basket. If the probability that the selected pear is ripe is $\\frac{1}{4},$  what proportion of the pears from basket $C$ are ripe?" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369077 1113 #uuid "4fc2267c-cf00-5edb-8fcf-dca3892e059b" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369077 1145 "Let $R$ be the event that the pear is ripe, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the pear comes from basket $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the pear comes from basket $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the pear comes from basket $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a ripe pear, we can use the law of total probability: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap B)+P(R\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since a basket is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(B)=P(C)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability of picking a ripe pear from baskets $A$ and $B$ are $\\frac{1}{6}$ and $\\frac{1}{5},$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\nP(R|A)=\\frac{1}{6},\\,P(R|B)=\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\n- Since the probability of picking a ripe pear from one of the baskets is $\\frac{1}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(R|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =(\\frac{1}{6})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{1}{5})(\\frac{1}{3})+P(R|C)(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{18}+\\frac{1}{15}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{18}-\\frac{1}{15} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) & =\\frac{23}{180} \\\\ P(R|C) & =\\frac{23}{60}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{23}{60}$ of the pears from basket $C$ are ripe." 13194139535835 true] [17592186369078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369079 13194139535835 true] [17592186369078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369080 13194139535835 true] [17592186369078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369081 13194139535835 true] [17592186369078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369082 13194139535835 true] [17592186369078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369083 13194139535835 true] [17592186369078 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535835 true] [17592186369078 1041 "selection" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369078 1104 #edb/ref 17592186369081 13194139535835 true] [17592186369078 1110 #uuid "ac69e96d-fec1-590b-97ef-264b252bbc4f" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369079 1034 "\\frac{24}{55}" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369079 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369079 1111 #uuid "2480cdbb-3eea-5c96-bb8f-edcdbea7e886" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369080 1034 "\\frac{17}{50}" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369080 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369080 1111 #uuid "0f6578c2-3e2a-59ca-9395-cf95e3481042" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369081 1034 "\\frac{23}{60}" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369081 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369081 1111 #uuid "2e97dc51-6646-5265-ace0-e86a9d3b1bb9" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369082 1034 "\\frac{18}{55}" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369082 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369082 1111 #uuid "c30e77af-1243-526a-9dd2-9fac58e17d39" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369083 1034 "\\frac{19}{62}" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369083 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369083 1111 #uuid "6adb2792-0256-5852-a7e2-ec880e34fbd4" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369084 1017 #edb/ref 17592186369085 13194139535835 true] [17592186369084 1089 "q-148669" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369084 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535835 true] [17592186369084 1100 "A local newspaper is sold by three distributors, $1,$ $2,$ and $3.$\n\n- Distributor $1$ gets $36%$ of the copies.\n\n- Distributor $2$ gets $23%$ of the copies, and $92%$ of those copies are sold.\n\n- Distributor $3$ gets $41%$ of the copies, and $95%$ of those copies are sold.\n\nIf $3%$ of all copies have some printing defect, $70%$ of defective copies are sold, and $2.5%$ of sold copies are defective, what is the probability that a randomly chosen copy bought by distributor $1$ is sold? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535835 true] [17592186369084 1113 #uuid "a8c2e20f-0f73-51af-a685-5cac68a35ca3" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369084 1145 "Let $R$ be the event that a copy has a printing defect, $S$ be the event that a copy is sold, and ${D}_{1},{D}_{2},{D}_{3}$ be the event that a copy is given to distributor $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({D}_{1})=0.36 \\\\  & P({D}_{2})=0.23 &  & P(S|{D}_{2})=0.92 \\\\  & P({D}_{3})=0.41 &  & P(S|{D}_{3})=0.95\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${D}_{1}⊔{D}_{2}⊔{D}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({D}_{1})+P({D}_{2})+P({D}_{3})=0.36+0.23+0.41=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $3%$ of all copies has some printing defect, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.03.\n$$\n\n- Since $70%$ of defective copies are sold, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|R)=0.7.\n$$\n\n- Since $2.5%$ of sold copies are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(R|S)=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen copy bought by distributor $1$ is sold, which is represented as $P(S|{D}_{1}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|S) & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S)} \\\\ P(R|S) & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S|{D}_{1})P({D}_{1})+P(S|{D}_{2})P({D}_{2})+P(S|{D}_{3})P({D}_{3})} \\\\ 0.025 & =\\frac{(0.7)(0.03)}{P(S|{D}_{1})(0.36)+(0.92)(0.23)+(0.95)(0.41)} \\\\ 0.025 & =\\frac{0.021}{0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(S|{D}_{1}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.025(0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011) & =0.021 \\\\ 0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011 & =\\frac{0.021}{0.025} \\\\ 0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011 & =\\frac{21}{25} \\\\ 0.36P(S|{D}_{1}) & =\\frac{21}{25}-0.6011 \\\\ P(S|{D}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{0.36}(\\frac{21}{25}-0.6011) \\\\ P(S|{D}_{1}) & ≈0.664\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535835 true] [17592186369085 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369086 13194139535835 true] [1759218