text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $4$ and $6,$ as shown above." 13194139535835 true] [17592186369091 1113 #uuid "7644e0cb-fe7e-5af8-a7f6-5b53f6f2ec86" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369091 1145 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the annulus between the disks of radii $4$ and $6$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ we obtain $r=4.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36,$ we obtain $r=6.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,4\\le r\\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{4}^{6}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{4}^{6}\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {[r]}_{4}^{6}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (6-4)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta }{3}]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369092 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369093 13194139535835 true] [17592186369092 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369094 13194139535835 true] [17592186369092 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369095 13194139535835 true] [17592186369092 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369096 13194139535835 true] [17592186369092 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369097 13194139535835 true] [17592186369092 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535835 true] [17592186369092 1041 "selection" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369092 1104 #edb/ref 17592186369096 13194139535835 true] [17592186369092 1110 #uuid "2cf89217-10fb-502b-bb7f-a7ab0f96783e" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369093 1034 "2" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369093 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369093 1111 #uuid "dd2024a9-31c2-5ab4-8f1b-5b3d44d87152" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369094 1034 "1" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369094 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369094 1111 #uuid "a0f485c8-8cd5-5a48-ab7e-35be33d34082" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369095 1034 "2\\pi " 13194139535835 true] [17592186369095 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369095 1111 #uuid "da0f71ac-ac79-53a5-8242-505aad22a02a" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369096 1034 "0" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369096 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369096 1111 #uuid "d5c05471-f12d-55e7-9901-267fe90e8ef4" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369097 1034 "4{\\pi }^{2}" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369097 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369097 1111 #uuid "254097d6-f674-5cee-b836-1f0fb8e188ae" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369098 1017 #edb/ref 17592186369099 13194139535835 true] [17592186369098 1089 "q-164830" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369098 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535835 true] [17592186369098 1100 "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =0, & \\,{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]$ to approximate the value of $f(1,1).$" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369098 1113 #uuid "8b9a0044-c09a-5774-bb14-17d82777ff05" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369098 1145 "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}],$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =0, & \\,\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}], & \\,\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈0+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & ≈0+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ -(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =-{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369099 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369100 13194139535835 true] [17592186369099 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369101 13194139535835 true] [17592186369099 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369102 13194139535835 true] [17592186369099 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369103 13194139535835 true] [17592186369099 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369104 13194139535835 true] [17592186369099 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535835 true] [17592186369099 1041 "selection" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369099 1104 #edb/ref 17592186369101 13194139535835 true] [17592186369099 1110 #uuid "1c3727fc-202d-57f2-ab46-0fca87ebaebe" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369100 1034 "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369100 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369100 1111 #uuid "1a11a37