ed cloudy morning continues as a rainy day?" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369147 1113 #uuid "c49a23bb-a7fc-57e9-921a-fc537e2e5220" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369147 1145 "Let $C$ be the event that a randomly selected morning is cloudy, and let $R$ be the event that a randomly selected day is rainy. Then, we require $P(R|C),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(R|C)=\\frac{P(C|R)P(R)}{P(C)}\n$$\n\n- $P(C)=0.3,$ since $30%$ of mornings are cloudy.\n\n- $P(R)=0.4,$ since $40%$ of days are rainy.\n\n- $P(C|R)=0.6,$ since $60%$ of rainy days start with cloudy mornings.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|C) & =\\frac{P(C|R)P(R)}{P(C)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.6\\cdot 0.4}{0.3} \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369148 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369149 13194139535835 true] [17592186369148 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369150 13194139535835 true] [17592186369148 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369151 13194139535835 true] [17592186369148 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369152 13194139535835 true] [17592186369148 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369153 13194139535835 true] [17592186369148 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535835 true] [17592186369148 1041 "selection" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369148 1104 #edb/ref 17592186369150 13194139535835 true] [17592186369148 1110 #uuid "ab5f0c6b-6959-594d-9567-93b7821e26a7" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369149 1034 "0.6" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369149 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369149 1111 #uuid "e78f7d83-8f03-50a7-98ca-15315cdaa1a4" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369150 1034 "0.8" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369150 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369150 1111 #uuid "5bf9317b-89ed-519e-93b9-193c5f3cd387" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369151 1034 "0.25" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369151 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369151 1111 #uuid "cc1b613e-f4b3-52f3-bf0c-27b40021a8c1" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369152 1034 "0.45" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369152 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369152 1111 #uuid "83a6ed1d-84b6-5db6-8099-6e8d5d474f50" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369153 1034 "0.9" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369153 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369153 1111 #uuid "6393cbad-9a82-5290-abe1-d2c63b65f26d" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369154 1017 #edb/ref 17592186369155 13194139535835 true] [17592186369154 1089 "q-65145" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369154 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535835 true] [17592186369154 1100 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}\n$$\n\n- The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$\n- The limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{2}$ along the path $y=-x$\n- The limit exists and is equal to $0$" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369154 1113 #uuid "b171da41-b950-5445-bd40-70968beeadd7" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369154 1145 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}=\\frac{2\\cdot 0+0}{3\\cdot 0+0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x+x}{3x+x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x}{4x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-2x+x}{3x-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-x}{2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369155 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369156 13194139535835 true] [17592186369155 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369157 13194139535835 true] [17592186369155 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369158 13194139535835 true] [17592186369155 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369159 13194139535835 true] [17592186369155 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369160 13194139535835 true] [17592186369155 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535835 true] [17592186369155 1041 "selection" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369155 1104 #edb/ref 17592186369160 13194139535835 true] [17592186369155 1110 #uuid "02a4ddfa-5f0b-5d3d-9cb6-b34db3ad5044" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369156 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369156 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535835 true] [17592186369156 1111 #uuid "06fce26b-7020-5f61-9971-36933a80e252" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369157 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369157 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535835 true] [17592186369157 1111 #uuid "2814d360-7194-570f-9f65-58f8bb9ca8cb" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369158 1034 "I only" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369158 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535835 true] [17592186369158 1111 #uuid "c8aa9617-31a4-53fc-8878-4b3f76800e82" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369159 1034 "III only" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369159 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535835 true] [17592186369159 1111 #uuid "7ddafe21-3567-526b-b2ae-185a6f8c0bec" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369160 1034 "II only" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369160 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535835 true] [17592186369160 1111 #uuid "7424d9fe-e76a-5a0e-b498-ea5cfa88774b" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369161 1017 #edb/ref 17592186369162 13194139535835 true] [17592186369161 1089 "q-73257" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369161 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535835 true] [17592186369161 1100 "Find the projection of the intersection between the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{12}=1$ and the plane $z-y=3$ onto the $xy$-plane." 13194139535835 true] [17592186369161 1113 #uuid "33ec3330-aeaf-5e9b-a359-02e080019320" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369161 1145 "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $z$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-y=3\\,⟹\\,z=y+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}+6y+9}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{12}+\\frac{{y}^{2}+6y+9}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{3{y}^{2}+6y+9}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}+2y+3}{4} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $y$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}+2y+3}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{({y}^{2}+2y+1)+2}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}+2}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{1}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3/2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3/2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2}=1.$" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369162 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369163 13194139535835 true] [17592186369162 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369164 13194139535835 true] [17592186369162 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369165 13194139535835 true] [17592186369162 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369166 13194139535835 true] [17592186369162 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369167 13194139535835 true] [17592186369162 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535835 true] [17592186369162 1041 "selection" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369162 1104 #edb/ref 17592186369166 13194139535835 true] [17592186369162 1110 #uuid "bd3832a9-d2dd-5ba0-87a9-c9aee6a7039e" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369163 1034 "\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2/3}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2}=1." 13194139535835 true] [17592186369163 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369163 1111 #uuid "da666821-a92c-58e8-ae5c-e9ca2f31d8c8" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369164 1034 "\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2/3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1/2}=1." 13194139535835 true] [17592186369164 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369164 1111 #uuid "53b1eaf4-603b-5176-9a44-cc72731259da" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369165 1034 "\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{1/2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3/2}=1." 13194139535835 true] [17592186369165 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369165 1111 #uuid "77809515-85bf-5517-861f-53ef115df517" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369166 1034 "\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3/2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2}=1." 13194139535835 true] [17592186369166 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369166 1111 #uuid "4c0fe807-be82-513c-af75-0c010b3822a7" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369167 1034 "\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3/2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{1/2}=1." 13194139535835 true] [17592186369167 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369167 1111 #uuid "f20531fc-cf32-5341-9c48-ff9deeae35c7" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369168 1017 #edb/ref 17592186369169 13194139535835 true] [17592186369168 1089 "q-79811" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369168 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535835 true] [17592186369168 1100 "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y)$ from $(0.5,1)$ to $(0,2).$" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369168 1113 #uuid "cfde08b6-0b33-56dc-ab2e-672049a30b4a" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369168 1145 "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2x\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(0.5,1),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(0.5,1) & =2\\cdot 0.5\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(0.5+Δx,1+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈1\\,Δx+1\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(0.5,1)$ to $(0,2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =0-0.5=-0.5, \\\\ Δy & =2-1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈1\\,Δx+1\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈1\\cdot (-0.5)+1\\cdot (1) \\\\  & ≈0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ increases by approximately $0.5.$" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369169 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369170 13194139535835 true] [17592186369169 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369171 13194139535835 true] [17592186369169 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369172 13194139535835 true] [17592186369169 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369173 13194139535835 true] [17592186369169 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369174 13194139535835 true] [17592186369169 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535835 true] [17592186369169 1041 "selection" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369169 1104 #edb/ref 17592186369170 13194139535835 true] [17592186369169 1110 #uuid "1d173091-98b1-5f9e-ba14-223fe159f379" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369170 1034 "0.5" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369170 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369170 1111 #uuid "e3d1a48d-b303-5108-bf3d-41cf2b142017" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369171 1034 "-1.5" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369171 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369171 1111 #uuid "3e591d12-74c2-5577-b196-484985280fbb" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369172 1034 "1.5" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369172 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369172 1111 #uuid "320ddc7c-7c7d-541e-9ebe-9ac46df72c2c" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369173 1034 "-0.5" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369173 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369173 1111 #uuid "2f8f7c53-d3cc-5bfe-9343-72ec3b72a943" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369174 1034 "1" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369174 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369174 1111 #uuid "542a575b-c995-5c99-8b0f-3f774e748289" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369175 1017 #edb/ref 17592186369176 13194139535835 true] [17592186369175 1089 "q-80778" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369175 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535835 true] [17592186369175 1100 "Consider the surface ${2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}=2.$ Given that $n=⟨a,b,c⟩$ is a non-zero normal vector to the surface at the point $(1,-1,1),$ what is the value of $\\frac{c}{b}?$" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369175 1113 #uuid "229858c7-5c8f-5030-8583-21587b3271c1" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369175 1145 "Let's define  $f(x,y,z)={2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=2.$  \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(1,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy})j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy})k \\\\  & =({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\cdot 2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)i+({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\cdot 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)j+({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\cdot 6z\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)k \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(y\\,\\text{ }{2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\text{ }i+x\\,\\text{ }{2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\text{ }j+3z\\,\\text{ }{2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\text{ }k) \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,1) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2((-1)\\cdot \\text{ }{2}^{3(1)^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}\\text{ }i+1\\cdot \\,\\text{ }{2}^{3(1)^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}\\text{ }j+3(1)\\cdot {2}^{3(1)^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}\\text{ }k) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(-{2}^{1}\\text{ }i+{2}^{1}\\text{ }j+3\\cdot {2}^{1}\\text{ }k) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(-i+j+3\\text{ }k) \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩=⟨-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{c}{b}=\\frac{12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}=3.$" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369176 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369177 13194139535835 true] [17592186369176 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369178 13194139535835 true] [17592186369176 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369179 13194139535835 true] [17592186369176 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369180 13194139535835 true] [17592186369176 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369181 13194139535835 true] [17592186369176 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535835 true] [17592186369176 1041 "selection" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369176 1104 #edb/ref 17592186369178 13194139535835 true] [17592186369176 1110 #uuid "6b31f61a-2eb7-5caa-8974-71cfeeb1b2f2" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369177 1034 "-5" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369177 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369177 1111 #uuid "0a46a3bc-9089-599a-a63f-9fc68a7eeeb7" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369178 1034 "3" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369178 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369178 1111 #uuid "38718a47-8236-522f-8bab-75d4a7aed904" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369179 1034 "-\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369179 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369179 1111 #uuid "e312aa09-d16e-5084-a55f-f0d28e042abf" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369180 1034 "4" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369180 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369180 1111 #uuid "ba8d3569-d110-5d71-8155-d4acf556257c" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369181 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369181 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369181 1111 #uuid "1e895897-5109-50c8-9869-355a5ce0aea0" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369182 1017 #edb/ref 17592186369183 13194139535835 true] [17592186369182 1089 "q-81740" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369182 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535835 true] [17592186369182 1100 "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=(y+1)\\,i+(x+z+1)\\,j+(y+1)\\,k.$" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369182 1113 #uuid "7429fa1e-a8b3-5b4c-87be-75aad7649b8b" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369182 1145 "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+z+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y+1\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (y+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =xy+x+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+z+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =x+z+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =z+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int (z+1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =yz+y+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=yz+y+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy+x+yz+y+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =y+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =1 \\\\ h(z) & =z+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy+yz+x+y+z+C.\n$$" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369183 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369184 13194139535835 true] [17592186369183 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369185 13194139535835 true] [17592186369183 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369186 13194139535835 true] [17592186369183 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369187 13194139535835 true] [17592186369183 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369188 13194139535835 true] [17592186369183 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535835 true] [17592186369183 1041 "selection" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369183 1104 #edb/ref 17592186369186 13194139535835 true] [17592186369183 1110 #uuid "333026f2-f23d-583e-938d-a19470e048a0" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369184 1034 "f(x,y,z)=xyz+xz+xy+yz+C" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369184 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369184 1111 #uuid "db422412-128c-551d-bfe7-009fa0d0a988" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369185 1034 "f(x,y,z)=xy+yz+xz+x+y+C" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369185 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369185 1111 #uuid "37c2d898-6a51-5075-9c1a-67842a6be680" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369186 1034 "f(x,y,z)=xy+yz+x+y+z+C" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369186 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369186 1111 #uuid "28f0ba0d-36db-5319-9d3d-a60c9cdd8b53" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369187 1034 "f(x,y,z)=xy+yz+xz+y+z+C" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369187 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369187 1111 #uuid "dfd4134d-765e-586f-851e-78f013e6d97a" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369188 1034 "f(x,y,z)=xyz+x+y+z+C" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369188 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369188 1111 #uuid "51a8a8d3-3021-5096-952b-ca349320a568" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369189 1017 #edb/ref 17592186369190 13194139535835 true] [17592186369189 1089 "q-82338" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369189 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535835 true] [17592186369189 1100 "Change the order of integration in the double integral \n${\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{x-2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369189 1113 #uuid "df7a9928-b595-5d80-8b20-2abd529873c6" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369189 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/75f9c68145ed74a2c3530012061eb65cd93bae38d82fa0d34e5dc042f8073841.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le x-2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny=x-2\\,⟹\\,x=y+2\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=4.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type II region. The type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,y-2\\le x\\le 4},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/575e7e482f88635f6267405f66857a5488dac79833fce16d3d998df19e24d119.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{x-2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y+2}^{4}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369190 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369191 13194139535835 true] [17592186369190 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369192 13194139535835 true] [17592186369190 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369193 13194139535835 true] [17592186369190 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369194 13194139535835 true] [17592186369190 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369195 13194139535835 true] [17592186369190 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535835 true] [17592186369190 1041 "selection" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369190 1104 #edb/ref 17592186369193 13194139535835 true] [17592186369190 1110 #uuid "c87e4d0f-6e2a-5ad3-977f-9b55fe24dd26" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369191 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{y+2}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369191 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369191 1111 #uuid "6789d9af-6700-51dc-b2d2-5bbe7ed0b42d" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369192 1034 "{\\int }_{y+2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369192 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369192 1111 #uuid "e97ce313-8c83-56a3-8144-1a2d40385944" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369193 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y+2}^{4}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369193 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369193 1111 #uuid "3fa40dda-3ced-5113-98cd-a86d6a837bcf" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369194 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{y+2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369194 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369194 1111 #uuid "07d44a2c-c9ca-59f6-b2b3-9b6d27a7ff0e" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369195 1034 "{\\int }_{y+2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369195 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369195 1111 #uuid "46cb55d5-8a50-5539-a5ab-94381bd4a411" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369196 1017 #edb/ref 17592186369197 13194139535835 true] [17592186369196 1089 "q-86471" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369196 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535835 true] [17592186369196 1100 "Consider the surface ${x}^{2}{y}^{4}+{e}^{yz}{z}^{3}=4.$ Given that $n=⟨a,b,c⟩$ is a non-zero normal vector to the surface at the point $(2,-1,0),$ what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369196 1113 #uuid "d36da64c-0744-5c6d-adbb-e4cbe16d0433" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369196 1145 "Let's define  $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{4}+{e}^{yz}{z}^{3}.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=4.$  \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(2,-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{4}+{e}^{yz}{z}^{3})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{4}+{e}^{yz}{z}^{3})j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}{y}^{4}+{e}^{yz}{z}^{3})k \\\\  & =(2x{y}^{4})i+(4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}+{z}^{4}{e}^{yz})j+({z}^{2}{e}^{yz}(yz+3))k \\\\ ∇f(2,-1,0) & =2(2)(-1)^{4}\\text{ }i+[4(2)^{2}(-1)^{3}+{0}^{4}{e}^{(-1)(0)}]\\text{ }j+[{0}^{2}{e}^{(-1)(0)}((-1)(0)+3)]\\text{ }k \\\\  & =4\\text{ }i-16\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,-16,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩=⟨4,\\,-16,\\,0⟩.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-16}{4}=-4.$" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369197 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369198 13194139535835 true] [17592186369197 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369199 13194139535835 true] [17592186369197 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369200 13194139535835 true] [17592186369197 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369201 13194139535835 true] [17592186369197 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369202 13194139535835 true] [17592186369197 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535835 true] [17592186369197 1041 "selection" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369197 1104 #edb/ref 17592186369202 13194139535835 true] [17592186369197 1110 #uuid "c05fc4c1-d7d4-542c-ad70-48a1a0bcff8d" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369198 1034 "-e" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369198 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369198 1111 #uuid "b804cd3b-2565-5b06-9eb6-ef89ff621a63" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369199 1034 "16" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369199 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369199 1111 #uuid "7713ced6-5f50-561f-b97b-b1226413d7d5" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369200 1034 "8" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369200 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369200 1111 #uuid "824a6509-df8e-5766-b650-e72371d6b7d8" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369201 1034 "4e" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369201 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369201 1111 #uuid "1607a6b1-fbb7-555b-9281-d7382dc69494" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369202 1034 "-4" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369202 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535835 true] [17592186369202 1111 #uuid "d047b765-74ce-5fad-ae09-fcf4764f9d78" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369203 1017 #edb/ref 17592186369204 13194139535835 true] [17592186369203 1089 "q-94377" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369203 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535835 true] [17592186369203 1100 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=32t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)+16t$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2t$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+8t$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." 13194139535835 true] [17592186369203 1113 #uuid "d9246e15-4875-5686-8139-62f5acf7f760" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369203 1145 "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={y}_{c}(t)+{y}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the simple harmonic motion equation ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.$ It can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,\\omega =4.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ Since the external force is a linear function, we assume that the particular solution is also a linear function,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta .\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =2$ and $\\beta =0.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2t, \\\\ y(t) & =R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2t, \\\\ y(t) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t+2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly II matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139535835 true] [17592186369204 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369205 13194139535835 true] [17592186369204 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369206 13194139535835 true] [17592186369204 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369207 13194139535835 true] [17592186369204 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369208 13194139535835 true] [17592186369204 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369209 13194139535835 true] [17592186369204 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535835 true] [17592186369204 1041 "selection" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369204 1104 #edb/ref 17592186369209 13194139535835 true] [17592186369204 1110 #uuid "8798d2d1-2c90-5c9f-8a4f-a8a92aba57df" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369205 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369205 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535835 true] [17592186369205 1111 #uuid "a485039c-7553-52e4-9f8d-b01f2b27c8ee" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369206 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369206 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535835 true] [17592186369206 1111 #uuid "5df26e7f-a36a-52de-9d2f-769e31017ea3" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369207 1034 "I only" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369207 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535835 true] [17592186369207 1111 #uuid "498192d5-ad48-5291-aab2-3b51e4c58401" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369208 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369208 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535835 true] [17592186369208 1111 #uuid "84e535f8-5cf3-50df-ae2d-852b3ca8abe7" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369209 1034 "II only" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369209 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535835 true] [17592186369209 1111 #uuid "86d403b6-3b13-5ebd-b33c-5fbba101e04c" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369210 1017 #edb/ref 17592186369211 13194139535835 true] [17592186369210 1089 "q-97078" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369210 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535835 true] [17592186369210 1100 "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps ${R}^{2}$ onto  ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{Im}(T)={0}$\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$\n- $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$, where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369210 1113 #uuid "b7b6dd53-171d-534b-b5b5-b5fe81eabd1b" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369210 1145 "Recall that a linear transformation $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto  ${R}^{2}$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=2\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The condition $\\text{rank}(T)=2$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the condition $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have that \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369211 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369212 13194139535835 true] [17592186369211 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369213 13194139535835 true] [17592186369211 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369214 13194139535835 true] [17592186369211 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369215 13194139535835 true] [17592186369211 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369216 13194139535835 true] [17592186369211 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535835 true] [17592186369211 1041 "selection" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369211 1104 #edb/ref 17592186369216 13194139535835 true] [17592186369211 1110 #uuid "0f70b5f7-3b71-5355-aff9-d8df21fbbbec" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369212 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369212 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535835 true] [17592186369212 1111 #uuid "389401a5-6afb-562c-9b74-51cde6742754" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369213 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369213 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535835 true] [17592186369213 1111 #uuid "794915b4-c2f3-5f27-8bf0-5af14901813f" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369214 1034 "I only" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369214 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535835 true] [17592186369214 1111 #uuid "068c9e7e-8f1e-5353-b607-c6b3e7cbdd1f" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369215 1034 "III only" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369215 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535835 true] [17592186369215 1111 #uuid "5373e557-c82a-5af5-843e-9b028b00e209" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369216 1034 "II only" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369216 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535835 true] [17592186369216 1111 #uuid "58a11bc8-a782-5f92-8920-c066e8821e8c" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369217 1017 #edb/ref 17592186369218 13194139535835 true] [17592186369217 1089 "q-98525" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369217 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535835 true] [17592186369217 1100 "Which of the following is the phase line of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+y-2?$" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369217 1113 #uuid "519b7e3f-5e6b-5321-a66f-0a3fdc3bd943" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369217 1145 "To draw the phase line of the differential equation, we proceed using the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation. \n\nIn this case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+y-2 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y+2) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=1.$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the intervals of $y$ for which $f(y)>0$ and $f(y)<0.$ \n\nConstructing a sign table, we get the following:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,1)$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (1,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-2,1).$\n\n**Step 3**: Draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/39ab4bfad5e05067d9e50979deb2a7699eb41aa87037fdf6e3a6d14a98dcfa19.png)" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369218 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369219 13194139535835 true] [17592186369218 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369220 13194139535835 true] [17592186369218 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369221 13194139535835 true] [17592186369218 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369222 13194139535835 true] [17592186369218 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369223 13194139535835 true] [17592186369218 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535835 true] [17592186369218 1041 "selection" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369218 1104 #edb/ref 17592186369222 13194139535835 true] [17592186369218 1110 #uuid "bd3ab181-a050-5268-94ed-94744cae622e" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369219 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/0b6a1907f045e737c1f136a47d941069efbfd23a389add17d1ae31cf336763b8.png" 13194139535835 true] [17592186369219 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535835 true